lisa-design:
- ci.rerun idempotency = per (key, head SHA) via an in-process, bounded (1024) rerunLog on the github Conn, NOT GitHub state: the rerun endpoints take no body, so no marker can carry the key. Durable replay is the dispatcher's Effect store (Effector path replays a recorded (task,key) before Apply; rule keys are per event). Rejected: (a) treating "latest attempt triggered by our bot" as a replay (that is per-App, not per-key, and would block a second key's legitimate rerun); (b) changing internal/server to fold the head SHA into effectID (extra Observe per call, store key change) - not needed: a forgotten replay still posts nothing while the reran runs are queued/running, since only completed runs are rerun.
- nothing-to-rerun is not remembered, so a later call with the same key reruns a run that has failed since. A partially failed op is not remembered either; its retry relists and skips runs already queued.
- Cancelled runs go straight to POST .../rerun (not try rerun-failed-jobs then fall back): deterministic, one call, matches the spec's test "cancelled uses /rerun".
- Token is {actions: write, pull_requests: read}: the PR GET needs pull_requests read on private repos. A 403 names "Actions: write" only when GitHub says "not accessible by integration" (or the mint is refused); other 403s (run too old, already running) keep GitHub's message, still ErrInvalid.
- runOrder (observe.go) is the one superseding comparator for checksOf (PR A) and latestRuns; ci.rerun groups by (workflow_id, event) like checksOf.
- ci.rerun is not restricted to the author identity in the catalog validator: the reviewer App simply lacks Actions, and the mint refuses with ErrInvalid naming Actions: write.
- The op constant lands in the same PR as its implementation (AGENTS.md prefers contracts in their own PR): the stack was specified as two PRs and PR A is unrelated to the contract.

